['specifically slide number 13, from BITS Pilani, Pilani Campus. The bottom right corner includes the text "CS F363 Compiler Construction."
BITS Pilani, Pilani Campus
Translation Schemes: An Example 
Consider the Translation Scheme for the L-Attributed Definition for “type declarations”:
D → T {L.in := T .type } L 
T → int {T .type :=int } 
T → real {T .type :=real } 
L → { L 1.in := L.in } L1, id {addtype(id.entry, L.in) } 
L → id {addtype(id.entry, L.in) }
CS F363 Compiler Construction
14']

response:
The type declaration translation scheme passes the type to the identifier list through the `L` rules. Specifically, when `L` is defined as `L1`, it sets `L.in` to itself, and when `L` is an `id`, it calls `addtype(id.entry, L.in)` to add the type to the identifier. This is detailed in the rules:

L → { L1.in := L.in } L1, id {addtype(id.entry, L.in) } 
L → id {addtype(id.entry, L.in) }

This is from slide 13 of the document "CS F363 Compiler Construction" at BITS Pilani, Pilani Campus.
